PROPOSED EXTENTIONS/IMPROVEMENTS ===============================================

1. There is an issue with force sequences:

'\$a01234'

Where the desired interpretetation is '\$a' followed by '01234' is
not possible. This can be fixed by limiting the number of digits
in each of hex(2), octal(3), and binary(8). Then it becomes:

'\$0a01234'

2. The "update" primitive can't work on a text file. However, an "append"
procedure, which would be defined as "update(f); position(length(f)+1)" on a 
binary file, would work on text. This is because the contents of a file
is coordinated for lines. The only thing that could complicate this is
systems (like CP/M) that have a specific end of file marker. However, even this
can be overcome.

3. We implement a restriction that external procedures and functions that take
procedure or function parameters, can only be called with a global level
procedure or function as a parameter. This allows implementation of stored
callbacks without worrying about access to non-existant local variables.
It is true that the module which implements the callback could violate this
by calling itself. However, there is no standard language construct for
creation of callback routines, so the callback implementor is already
a specialized module, with assembly or type escapes. We could well have the
rule that procedure and function parameters not be allowed to cross module
lines.

4. Unsigned integer support.

5. Double precision integer support.

6. Implement complex modularity. See "complex.txt".

7. Implement readback of types in intermediate file. In order to do this,
we need to be able to check and match the date/time on a file to determine
if the source file has a more recent date/time than the .int file. If so,
we would revert to the source file. This should also be a mode by "safe"
option. [this will be part of the complex modularity fix]

8. Oberon implements case constant ranges, so I guess we are too.

9. Propose we allow "max" to give the max of any type, and add "min"
that can do the same. max(integer) = maxint. These functions work on
variables and types, including the current case of arrays. max(real)
and min(real) give floating point results, as well as max(sreal) and
min(sreal).

10. Expression operators shr (shift right) and shl (shift left) operators.

11. The predefined procedure "assert", such:

assert(expr)

If the assertion fails, it becomes a runtime error:

*** Runtime: Assert fails

It can also be evaluated for constant expression during compile time.

12. A general array can be useful in fixed declarations:

fixed a: array of char = 'hi there bob';

The actual demensions of the array are taken from the supplied data,
and so don't have to be specified directly as now.

13. Implement a "dma" or Direct Machine Access option.

14. Under the /dma option, the general function datatype(x) will convert any
ordinal or pointer value to the ordinal or pointer type result. It won't
convert reals to integer or vice versa.
[about all this does we can't do now is convert to and from pointers.]

15. Under /dma option, the function ptr(x) will perform a variable reference
to x, and return a pointer that is (like nil) compatible with any other
pointer type.

16. Under /dma option, the function size(x) will perform a variable reference
to x, and return the size, in bytes, of the data that is referenced. The
result is type integer.


